<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor etching''' removes exposed material from a wafer while a patterned mask protects areas intended to remain. The result may be a trench, hole, or isolated feature; the choice of chemistry and equipment determines how closely the etched shape follows the mask.</onlyinclude>

[[Semiconductor etching/Technical|Read the technical explanation]]

== How it works ==
In '''wet etching''', liquid chemicals react with or dissolve exposed material. Wet processes can be highly selective and relatively simple, but some etch in several directions, widening features beneath a mask. Certain crystalline materials instead etch at different speeds along different crystal planes.

In '''dry etching''', reactive gases or a plasma generate species that remove material, often as volatile products. In reactive-ion etching, energetic ions can promote preferential removal toward the bottom of a feature, enabling steeper sidewalls. Directionality depends on the chemistry, surface passivation, ion energy, and geometry; not every dry etch is anisotropic.

== Why engineers care ==
* '''Selectivity:''' how much faster the target layer is removed than a mask or underlying stop layer.
* '''Anisotropy:''' whether removal is preferentially vertical rather than lateral.
* '''Damage and residues:''' energetic ions, charging, contamination, and reaction products can degrade device surfaces.
* '''Uniformity:''' the etch must behave consistently across a wafer and within differently sized features.

A photolithographic pattern defines where removal is intended; etching performs the material removal. Film deposition adds material instead. Etching can also remove material from the mask or underlying layer, so a process that looks precise can still damage the intended structure.

